WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:04.003
Our first guest tonight is
an Academy Award-winning actor

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.839
and "New York Times"
best-selling author

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.640
you know from films
such as "Interstellar"

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:08.741
and "Dallas Buyers Club."

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:11.411
He returns as Buster Moon
in "Sing 2,"

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.846
which premieres
in theaters December 22nd.

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:15.181
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.083
-[ Bell dings ]
-[ Gasps ]

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:19.552
-Maybe it's like room service?

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.021
-[ Banging ]
-Open up or you're dead, Moon!

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:24.357
-Okay, it's not room service.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:27.092
-You and your dumb friends
better not be hiding in there!

00:00:27.092 --> 00:00:28.294
-What are we gonna do?

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.728
-Guys, we just
got to be brave now.

00:00:29.728 --> 00:00:31.730
-Are you saying we should
fight these thugs?

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.399
-No, no.
They'll beat us to a pulp.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:34.834
-[ Bang ]
-[ All gasp ]

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.770
-We're gonna put this show on,
whether Crystal likes it or not!

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.640
But first...we're gonna
jump out that window.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.441
-What?!

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:44.777
-I'm beginning
to like this guy.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:48.114
-Please welcome back to the show
Matthew McConaughey, everybody!

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:49.615
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:56.756
♪♪

00:00:56.756 --> 00:01:03.897
♪♪

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.065
-Alright.
-Welcome back!

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:08.101
-How you doing, Seth?
-There's a -- You know,

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.236
over the years,
I've come to expect

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:13.940
a certain level of excellence
from you in film.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.374
-Okay.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:17.943
-But, you know, now you are --

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:19.712
This is the first time that I've
been sitting across from you

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.413
where you are a "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:23.383
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:24.917
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:26.986
-Let me tell ya. I said it
a little bit backstage.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:30.222
There was this lull where
I think people were looking

00:01:30.222 --> 00:01:31.524
for exactly the book you wrote.

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:32.891
You couldn't have known that,

00:01:32.891 --> 00:01:34.360
probably, when you were
sitting down to write it,

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.861
'cause I think you probably
wrote a story authentic to you.

00:01:35.861 --> 00:01:37.096
You wrote about your life.

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:38.897
-And then COVID came along,
and we said,

00:01:38.897 --> 00:01:41.200
"Ooh, our release date
is October. COVID is coming."

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.935
There was talk about
moving the date.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:44.671
The elections were there.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:46.139
My feeling and the team's
feeling was, "Look.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:48.141
Let's lean into it,
with elections,

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.376
with all the mud-slinging,
with COVID.

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:52.411
People are gonna want
some good news."

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:53.580
You know what I mean?

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.448
People were gonna want to hear
some things

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.750
that are about the approach
to life, not the product.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.585
The process
and the approach to life

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:02.222
and how to get some satisfaction
not only for me,

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:03.722
that I think we can all get.

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:06.025
-And I think that everybody,
having watched you

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:08.360
over the years, they thought,
"Man, if I could get in

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.396
on Matthew McConaughey's
approach to life,

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:13.166
I think I'd be
in a lot better place."

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:14.834
-I hope so.
Look, it's not all mine.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.102
I've stolen a lot of stuff.
You know what I mean?

00:02:17.102 --> 00:02:19.339
-It does sound so much
better coming from you.

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:21.273
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.273 --> 00:02:24.143
-Ah. Yeah.
Yeah, the old voice.

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:26.712
The old voice makes the medicine
taste better sometimes.

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:29.415
-I heard from a lot of people
they read it and loved it.

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.651
And then I heard from equally,
if not more, people,

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.052
if there's anything you want

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.688
to listen to the audio of,
it is "Greenlights."

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.190
It's Matthew's book,
that you read.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.757
And how was it actually --
You know,

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:40.259
obviously,
writing it is one thing,

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:41.794
but then
when you got to read it --

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:43.062
-Then I got to
sort of perform it.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.430
And there's a lot of
different voices

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.831
'cause of my travels
around the world.

00:02:45.831 --> 00:02:47.199
I'm doing the voices of...

00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:49.669
[Australian accent]
Australians along the way.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.270
There's a great story
in there called

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.771
"Sydney
in the Rear-view Mirror."

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:54.907
It's a great black comedy.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:56.676
[Normal voice]
But I actually -- You know,

00:02:56.676 --> 00:03:00.046
I thought that we'd scheduled
four days to record it.

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:03.516
And I recorded it
in eight hours one day. Whoop.

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:04.784
-Wow.
-And I just --

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.352
Because I've told the stories
so many times,

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:07.653
I knew where the characters
came up

00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:09.221
and what the intentions were
and sort of how

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:11.857
the act 1, act 2, act 3
of each story was set up.

00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:13.626
And it was a ball doing it,
and we knocked it out,

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:14.961
and people are saying
they enjoy it.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:16.362
-And then,
when you were recording it,

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:17.897
was there ever anything
where you thought,

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:19.866
"Oh, I wish I'd written it
a different way"

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:21.534
or was it just,
"No, this is great"?

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:25.771
-No, by that time, I mean,
I had spent over a year editing,

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:29.275
so every punctuation,
every font,

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:31.911
everything in the book
was exactly as I wanted it.

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:34.614
So there was no going back.

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:36.382
And I didn't want to go back.
-Perfect.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:37.683
-So it was good.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:40.119
-That seems like
another piece of wisdom.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.555
-"There's no going back.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.757
And I couldn't go back.
And I didn't want to go back."

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.692
-Another thing that's changed
since the last time I saw you --

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:50.329
you are now a football-club
owner, but European soccer.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:52.331
You own a soccer team now.
Here you go.

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:53.666
Austin. Is it Austin FC?

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:55.534
-Austin FC.
The Austin Football Club.

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:57.937
-Now, I realize --
-Verde! Listos!

00:03:57.937 --> 00:03:59.572
-I realize that I put this up

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.240
like I was about to show
a soccer team,

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.310
and, instead, it's just you
with a bongo drum of sorts.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.578
-This is a djembe.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:08.348
This is our opening game
at Q2 Stadium.

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:09.715
-Alright.

00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:12.818
-And this suit is exactly
our colors of our team.

00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:15.721
I got this suit made
three years ago,

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:16.823
and this is
less than a year ago,

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:19.425
so I held onto this suit
for this night,

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:21.261
to come out with my djembe.

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:23.662
Bring your drum.
Drum is our instrument.

00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:25.698
Actually, we'll allow
any instrument in the stadium.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.633
But it was --
We have a chant, "Verde,"

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:29.802
which is Spanish for "green."

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:32.371
And the response to that
is, "Listos,"

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:34.139
which is Spanish for "ready."

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.008
So, the whole crowd,
I got them going.

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:40.679
Going, "Verde." "Listos."
"Verde." "Listos."

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:44.584
And the only challenge with this
night was, two years later,

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:46.219
hoping that that suit
would fit me,

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:47.920
'cause I hadn't tried it on
in two years.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.455
-And how'd it go?
-It worked out.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:50.956
-There you go.

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:52.891
And let me say, I think anyone
who's a fan of a team

00:04:52.891 --> 00:04:55.695
would just want some enthusiasm
from their owner,

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:58.063
and I think they got
what they wanted

00:04:58.063 --> 00:05:00.599
when that's your owner
right there.

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:03.636
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.005
So, we showed a clip
from "Sing."

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:07.940
This is "Sing 2."
Five years between the films.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.975
You've got three kids.
They're a little bit older.

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:12.011
Have they grown into
being a "Sing" audience?

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:13.746
Have they grown out of
being a "Sing" audience?

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:15.548
Are they just thrilled
to hear their dad's voice?

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:17.182
-They're still
in the "Sing" audience.

00:05:17.182 --> 00:05:21.920
Look, the first one was
basically one of the first films

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.490
I've ever done that they could
be an audience member to.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.024
-Yeah.
-I mean, for years --

00:05:26.024 --> 00:05:28.628
That's one of the reasons
I did it, 'cause it was like,

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:30.129
"What's your kids's
favorite movie you've done?"

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:32.231
I was like, "They haven't
seen any of them.

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:34.867
I haven't sat them down in
front of 'True Detective' yet."

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.369
It's gonna open up
a lot of questions.

00:05:36.369 --> 00:05:37.803
-You're like,
"This is gonna answer

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:39.038
a lot of questions about life."

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:41.841
-So, we give them
"Sing 2," "Sing 1."

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:43.943
They dig it.
I'm cooler around the --

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:46.245
I got some street cred at the
dinner table after "Sing 1."

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:49.581
"Sing 2," they were around
the process of making it.

00:05:49.581 --> 00:05:50.883
They were around
the process of recording.

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:52.852
They toured the studios in Paris

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:54.586
and got to know Garth,
the director.

00:05:54.586 --> 00:05:58.290
And they weren't as surprised
to see my voice

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.225
come out of a koala's head.
-Yeah.

00:06:00.225 --> 00:06:01.594
-But they still were
a little surprised.

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:03.128
I remember sitting there,
even with "Sing 2" --

00:06:03.128 --> 00:06:04.330
I'm sitting where you are.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.664
My kids are right here
to the right.

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.233
Buster Moon comes on the screen,
and my voice comes out.

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:09.168
They look at Buster.
They look at me.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:10.603
They look at Buster.
They look at me.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.004
They look at Buster.
They look at me.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:13.172
And, all of a sudden, they go,
"Ha ha!"

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:15.741
And I go, "Yeah." Yeah.

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:17.175
-What about --
This is -- You know,

00:06:17.175 --> 00:06:19.278
obviously, it's very exciting
that you're in this film,

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:21.380
don't get me wrong, but Bono.
-Bono!

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:24.816
-Making his animated-film debut.
-Graced us with his presence.

00:06:24.816 --> 00:06:26.953
-Yeah. That's really exciting.
-Really badass.

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:29.888
-Who does Bono play in the film?
-He plays Clay Calloway.

00:06:29.888 --> 00:06:35.094
He's the rock 'n' roller
who left the gig,

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.996
who had a personal tragedy
that took him away

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:38.498
from music,
never wanted to perform again.

00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:41.233
So, in that way, it's a story
of resurrection and rebirth.

00:06:41.233 --> 00:06:44.270
We have to go get him
to bring him from his refuge

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.438
to get him on the stage
in our show,

00:06:46.438 --> 00:06:48.440
if our show
is gonna go on at all.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.142
And if it doesn't go on,

00:06:50.142 --> 00:06:52.377
I'll probably get killed
by that wolf you saw.

00:06:52.377 --> 00:06:53.980
-No spoilers, no spoilers.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.316
-So we got to pull him out
and get him on the stage.

00:06:57.316 --> 00:06:58.684
-You got any holiday plans?
-Yes.

00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:00.119
-Alright.
What do you got going on?

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:01.420
-Going to Texas.
-Okay.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:03.756
-But this time,
we have my wife,

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.023
Camila's, family coming in.

00:07:05.023 --> 00:07:06.658
-Great.
-So, it's been four years,

00:07:06.658 --> 00:07:08.394
I think,
since we've all been together.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:11.497
So Christmas holidays
will be extra family.

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:13.265
Air mattresses will be blown up.

00:07:13.265 --> 00:07:16.068
-Okay.
-Three -- Some twin beds.

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:17.703
And it will be some late nights,

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:20.540
because the Brazilians do their
Christmas on Christmas Eve.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.207
-Okay.
-So you eat late.

00:07:22.207 --> 00:07:25.411
Then you open presents
at midnight, which --

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:27.746
Then, the next day,
you're opening presents all day,

00:07:27.746 --> 00:07:29.681
'cause my mom likes to see
every single present

00:07:29.681 --> 00:07:31.717
opened up one by one.

00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:34.286
And my brother's coming,

00:07:34.286 --> 00:07:36.222
who loves to just put meat
on the smoker.

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:38.090
Not because he loves the way
it tastes.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.059
Just because it takes
longer to cook.

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:42.661
And he figures
if you're eating by midnight,

00:07:42.661 --> 00:07:44.229
no matter what it tastes like,

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:45.864
everyone likes it
'cause they're so damn hungry.

00:07:45.864 --> 00:07:47.232
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:47.232 --> 00:07:49.035
-Oh, that's a very smart move
for a chef, yeah.

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:50.602
-Yeah.

00:07:50.602 --> 00:07:52.438
-My thought of people who run
the smoker is always

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:53.906
they just want to use it
as an excuse

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:55.274
for not having to do
anything else.

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:57.843
-That's it.
"Hey, I'm tending the brisket."

00:07:57.843 --> 00:08:00.446
"What are you doing?"

00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:02.415
"I just checked it.
Checked it an hour ago.

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:05.417
I better not walk away, though."

00:08:05.417 --> 00:08:07.486
-"Could dry right out."
-Yeah.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:09.488
-I want to congratulate you
on something.

00:08:09.488 --> 00:08:12.024
"Hollywood Reporter" named you
Philanthropist of the Year.

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.893
Congratulations on that.
That must mean a lot.

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:15.094
-Yeah. Thank you. Thank you.

00:08:15.094 --> 00:08:16.896
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:16.896 --> 00:08:19.798
-Your organization, foundation,
just keep livin, is it --

00:08:19.798 --> 00:08:21.400
You've been doing this
for a while now.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:22.635
-We've been doing that 12 years.

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:24.303
-12 years.
-Which Camila and I founded it.

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:27.339
And we help at-risk student --

00:08:27.339 --> 00:08:29.508
high-school students
in Title I schools in America.

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:31.977
We're in over 40 schools now.
They meet twice a week.

00:08:31.977 --> 00:08:34.113
It's a safe place for them
to go after school.

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:37.817
They make a physical-fitness
goal, whether that's,

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:40.219
"I want to make the soccer team,
so I need to get in shape"

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:42.121
or whether that's,
"I need to lose 3 pounds

00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:44.657
so I can fit in my prom dress,"
we'll help them meet that goal.

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:48.226
Nutritional goals.
They do community service.

00:08:48.226 --> 00:08:50.496
They all do community service
in their communities.

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:52.999
And the halo
over the whole foundation,

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:54.667
which is really cool,
is gratitude.

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:56.802
We all sit around
after every class,

00:08:56.802 --> 00:08:59.471
and everyone sits in a circle
and has to share,

00:08:59.471 --> 00:09:02.307
out loud, something they are
thankful for in their life.

00:09:02.307 --> 00:09:04.243
And that's kind of
the coolest part about it.

00:09:04.243 --> 00:09:05.577
-That's really great.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:09:05.577 --> 00:09:07.013
-I know there had been
some talk --

00:09:07.013 --> 00:09:09.048
You know, obviously, you're
making a difference with that.

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:10.716
There was some talk
about you maybe embarking

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:12.685
on a political career, maybe
running for governor of Texas.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:14.186
You've since said
you're not gonna do that.

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:16.055
-Yep.
-Was that something that --

00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:18.323
Was your family on the side of,
"hey, maybe that's not for you,"

00:09:18.323 --> 00:09:22.294
or were they disappointed
you decided not to do it?

00:09:22.294 --> 00:09:23.929
-My daughter was
a little disappointed.

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:25.665
She thought
it would be really cool.

00:09:25.665 --> 00:09:27.900
But she's 11. I mean, she --

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.636
-Yeah, not quite
campaign-manager level.

00:09:30.636 --> 00:09:34.373
-Wasn't ready to hire her
for that position yet.

00:09:34.373 --> 00:09:37.910
The family -- Look, all
my decisions now going forward,

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.646
I share them
with my wife, Camila.

00:09:40.646 --> 00:09:41.980
She knows me as well as anybody.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:43.315
She asks me the hard questions.

00:09:43.315 --> 00:09:45.785
She makes sure that
I'm convicted the right ways

00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:47.619
within the decisions
I'm gonna make.

00:09:47.619 --> 00:09:50.222
They were --
would have been on with it.

00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:52.591
They were ready
if that was what Popeye,

00:09:52.591 --> 00:09:55.127
or what Matthew,
was really wanting to do.

00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:56.561
So we discussed it
for quite a long time,

00:09:56.561 --> 00:09:58.230
and she knew
I was seriously considering.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.065
The whole family knew
I was seriously considering it.

00:10:00.065 --> 00:10:01.700
It got to the point
where I said,

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.969
"You know what? No."

00:10:03.969 --> 00:10:08.373
After living in a mind of
"maybe I will" for over a year,

00:10:08.373 --> 00:10:11.143
I said, "No, it's not
the right time for me right now.

00:10:11.143 --> 00:10:12.611
It's not my embassy.

00:10:12.611 --> 00:10:14.113
I'm gonna see what I can do

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:16.515
more in an informal space
as a leader."

00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:18.550
-Well, I'm not worried
about you

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:20.151
making a difference
no matter where you are.

00:10:20.151 --> 00:10:21.787
And I really appreciate
you spending time with us today.

00:10:21.787 --> 00:10:23.055
Thank you so much.
-Seth, I appreciate it.

00:10:23.055 --> 00:10:24.523
-That's Matthew McConaughey.

00:10:24.523 --> 00:10:27.927
"Sing 2" opens in theaters
December 22nd.

